<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nunnery Quadrangle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nunnery Quadrangle is a group of four long buildings around a courtyard at the Maya city of Uxmal in Yucatán, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, built around 900. Spanish visitors gave it its name, imagining a convent, though its purpose may have been residential or administrative. Its upper façades bear elaborate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puuc">Puuc</a> stone mosaics of latticework, feathered serpents, masks and miniature huts.</p></div>
